      "template": "The operational impact is {1}, {2}, {3} and {4}.\nThe service context is {8}, {9}, {10} and {11}.\nThe incident picture is {12}, {13}, {14} and {15}.",
      "prompt": "You are an experienced crisis-management and cyber-continuity trainer for fire, police, health, municipal and essential-service organisations. Write a realistic exercise or decision-support briefing of about 400 words for incident commanders and continuity leads, using selected situation values as evidence rather than assumptions. Describe the incident, affected services, dependencies, operational impacts, uncertainty, escalation and near-term pressures in a clear chronological structure. Distinguish confirmed facts from working hypotheses and avoid technical attack instructions. If continuity, containment, command, communication, recovery or investigation measures are listed, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects, and name important gaps or safeguards. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution. Conclude with exactly two reflection or exercise questions about service priorities, coordination, evidence or public confidence.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Cyber incident and continuity exercise",
        "desc": "<p>A developing cyber incident and continuity incident is characterised by Healthcare, Accessible via the internet, External supplier, Residents and clients, Complete outage of the digital service, Coordination during the incident, Internal monitoring, Working hypothesis under investigation, Via a public online portal, Not available digitally, Reliability still uncertain, Recovery not yet started. Early reports do not yet establish its full scope or cause. The incident lead verifies immediate exposure with service owners and partner agencies, records confirmed facts separately from working assumptions, and protects people who may be at risk.</p><p>Response teams maintain a shared picture of impacts, dependencies and available capacity. They select Use controlled manual registration, Isolate affected systems under incident lead, Rebuild priority services in a segregated environment, Convene the organisation-wide crisis team, Maintain a shared situational picture, Publish impacts and safe alternative access routes to support safe operations and communicate verified information through appropriate channels. Measures are sequenced as conditions change, with attention to service continuity, evidence integrity and public confidence.</p><p>Investigation measures include Preserve relevant logs and incident records, Engage competent cyber incident and forensic specialists, Refer evidence to law enforcement for investigation. Competent authorities coordinate attribution and lawful tracing without premature conclusions, and pursue apprehension, disruption or prosecution through appropriate routes. The exercise tests how operational leaders balance immediate protection, restoration and accountability as new information emerges.</p>",
        "choices": [
          "sector.zorg",
          "netwerk.internet",
          "afhankelijkheid.leverancier",
          "gebruikers.inwoners",
          "effect.uitval",
          "fase.respons",
          "detectie.monitoring",
          "zekerheid.werkhypothese",
          "kanaal.online",
          "niveau.offline",
          "gegevens.onzeker",
          "herstel.niet",
          "noodproces.handmatig",
          "cyber-containment.1",
          "cyber-recovery.2",
          "regie.organisatie",
          "afstemming.gezamenlijk",
          "publiek.dienst",
          "cyber-evidence.1",
          "cyber-attribution.1",
          "cyber-enforcement.1"
        ]
      }
    },
