      "template": "Flooding is driven by {1}; the water is {2} and {3}.\nThe affected area is {4}; {5} people and {6} care needs shape exposure.\nAccess is {7}, responders arrive by {8}, and essential services are {9}.",
      "prompt": "You are an experienced European crisis-management trainer for fire, police, medical, water-authority and municipal services. Write an approximately 400-word, realistic exercise scenario and decision-support briefing about river, coastal or pluvial flooding, using all selected situation values without inventing precise forecasts. Establish what is observed, what remains uncertain, who and which essential services are exposed, and how access may change. If measures are listed, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects, and identify important gaps; if no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution. Distinguish immediate life safety from stabilisation and recovery. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions for the multi-agency team.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Rising water reaches the village",
        "desc": "<p>After sustained rainfall and high river discharge, water levels are expected to rise and streets in a rural village begin to flood. One neighbourhood may be affected, including home-care clients who live across the area. Some roads remain passable, but response teams are taking an alternative route as access conditions change.</p><p>Local restrictions affect essential services, including communications. The regional meeting brings the municipality and water authority together to agree a shared picture, while staff prepare accessible temporary shelter and publish regular public updates. Residents are asked to follow official information and report urgent access or care needs through established channels.</p><p>With water rescue teams available, responders discuss a phased road evacuation should conditions worsen. They also consider restoring communications at affected sites and coordinating outreach to home-care clients. The exercise asks when action should move from preparation to evacuation, how to avoid leaving people with care needs behind, and what information would justify scaling up beyond the local response.",
        "choices": [
          "bron.rivier",
          "verloop.stijgend",
          "beeld.straat",
          "gebied.dorp",
          "inwoners.wijk",
          "zorgvraag.verspreid",
          "wegen.deels",
          "aanvoer.omweg",
          "voorziening.plaatselijk",
          "opvang.lokaal",
          "regie.regio",
          "warning.public-update",
          "evacuation.phased",
          "rescue.boat",
          "service-continuity.service-comms"
        ]
      }
    },
