      "template": "The natural area is {1}, the fire status is {2}, and conditions are {3}.\nSmoke is {4}; {5} people are present near {6}.\nThe observed fire extent is {7}, with {8} confidence and {9}.",
      "prompt": "You are an experienced European fire-service and crisis-management exercise designer addressing incident commanders, land managers, police, health services and municipalities. Write an approximately 400-word realistic wildfire exercise scenario using every selected situation value, including the observed fire extent, smoke, people exposed and information gaps. Do not predict fire spread or prescribe tactical firefighting. If measures are listed, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects, and identify important gaps; if no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution. Consider public access, health advice, coordination and recovery as appropriate. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions for the multi-agency team.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Smoke near the heathland",
        "desc": "<p>A localised wildfire is confirmed in heathland after a period of dry weather and strong winds. Smoke is visible nearby, where recreational visitors are using facilities close to the area. Initial reports indicate a small, contained fire, but the incident commander notes that the response window is short and the full picture is still being verified.</p><p>The site manager and regional crisis partners coordinate with the municipality. The affected sector is closed while guided exit routes help visitors leave safely; additional crews and water supplies are pre-positioned without prescribing firefighting tactics. Public messages explain which routes are closed, where visitors can seek assistance and how updates will be provided.</p><p>Nearby accommodation providers ask about possible smoke exposure. The response team prepares health information for anyone affected, while reopening is deferred until the land manager can confirm that access is safe. The exercise asks how the team will verify who remains in the area, how it will keep warnings consistent, and what evidence is needed before reopening or recovery work begins.",
        "choices": [
          "terrein.heide",
          "status.lokaal",
          "omstandigheden.wind",
          "rook.plaatselijk",
          "mensen.recreanten",
          "omgeving.zorg",
          "fire-extent.small",
          "beeld.eerste",
          "response-window.short",
          "opvang.gemeente",
          "partners.regio",
          "nazorg.gezondheid",
          "afsluiting.deels",
          "toegang.geleid",
          "fire-support.preposition"
        ]
      }
    },
