      "template": "The demonstration concerns {1}, takes the format {2}, occurs at the stage {3}, and has the notification status {4}.\nThe setting is {5}, movement is {6}, surrounding access is {7}, and expected attendance is {8}.\nOrganiser contact is {9}; the operational priority is {10}; residents receive {11}; the observed atmosphere is {12}.",
      "prompt": "You are an experienced crisis-management trainer for police, fire, medical, municipal and diplomatic-protection partners. Write an approximately 400-word professional British-English exercise scenario for incident commanders and civil-protection planners. Describe the demonstration context, format, route, attendance and observed atmosphere, distinguishing verified facts from uncertainty. Respect the right to peaceful assembly and make facilitation, dialogue and proportionate safeguards the starting point; do not infer threat from whether participants are students, farmers or another group, and account for diplomatic premises where relevant. If public-order or investigation measures are listed, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies, side effects and important gaps. Investigation must concern specific alleged offences and distinguish individuals from peaceful participants. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution. Use a concise situation overview, developing impacts and decision points, then an assessment. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions.",
      "example": {
        "label": "A tense but peaceful embassy demonstration",
        "desc": "<p>Hundreds of students and farmers arrive for demonstrations in a neighbourhood with diplomatic premises. The student group plans a march along a previously discussed route, while farmers arrive with tractors for a stationary gathering nearby. The events are known to the authorities, organiser contacts are reachable and the gatherings begin peacefully before becoming tense but remain non-violent.</p><p>Temporary diversions are arranged while ordinary residents and emergency services retain access. The municipality provides local information and opens dialogue between organisers and relevant embassy security liaison staff. The right to demonstrate remains the starting point; participants are not treated as a threat because of their cause or identity.</p><p>Facilitation arrangements provide steward liaison and an accessible viewing route. Regular check-ins and first-aid support help sustain a calm event, while joint agency briefings and verified route updates support coordination. No offence is reported, so investigation and apprehension measures are not activated; the plan records how specific evidence-led action would be handled if an offence occurred.</p>",
        "choices": [
          "thema.studie",
          "vorm.mars",
          "fase.besluit",
          "kennisgeving.ontvangen",
          "locatie.diplomatiek",
          "route.afgesproken",
          "bereikbaarheid.omleiding",
          "opkomst.middel",
          "contact.bereikbaar",
          "belang.dialoog",
          "publiek.lokaal",
          "atmosphere.tense-but-non-violent",
          "assembly-facilitation.steward-liaison-support",
          "dialogue.regular-organiser-check-ins",
          "welfare.first-aid-point-available",
          "traffic.temporary-traffic-diversions",
          "response.visible-reassurance-patrols",
          "communications.embassy-security-liaison",
          "evidence.record-witness-accounts",
          "suspect-tracing.corroborate-witness-leads",
          "lawful-intervention.separate-offending-from-peaceful-assembly"
        ]
      }
