      "template": "Tsunami characteristics: source mechanism {1}; warning lead time {2}; inundation extent {3}.\nLocation and exposure: coastal setting {4}; occupancy {5}; evacuation routes {6}.\nImpact and capacity: water impact {7}; casualty burden {8}; response capacity {9}.",
      "prompt": "You are an experienced crisis-management trainer for fire, police, medical and municipal services. Write an approximately 400-word realistic tsunami incident narrative for professional exercise and decision-support use, based only on the supplied situation. Structure it with the source and warning timeline, exposed coastal population and routes, wave and infrastructure impacts, response capacity and uncertainties, then the operational picture. If measures are listed, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects, and identify important gaps; explicitly account for evacuation completion, successive waves and safe responder access. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions. Use clear international British English and avoid treating a small first wave as an all-clear.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Tsunami warning at a coastal harbour",
        "desc": "<p>An offshore earthquake has generated a tsunami threat to a European harbour and adjacent low-lying coastal plain. The first hazardous waves are forecast within tens of minutes, but inundation has not yet been observed. It is a peak visitor period, and the main road to higher ground is becoming congested while remaining open.</p><p>Local authorities have received official alerts and are confirming the exposed zone with port operators. Emergency services are available but must keep shore-based responders outside the predicted flow path. The possibility of later, larger waves means that an initial recession or small wave cannot be treated as an all-clear.</p><p>Measures selected include evacuation of the inundation zone, swift-water teams held for safe deployment after inundation, triage points prepared for casualties, cell-broadcast alerts and sirens, road closures into the hazard area, and local multi-agency command. Public messages emphasise high ground, staying away from the waterfront and awaiting an official all-clear.</p>",
        "choices": [
          "ts-source.earthquake",
          "ts-lead.tens",
          "ts-inundation.none",
          "ts-coast.harbour",
          "ts-occupancy.peak",
