      "prompt": "You are an experienced crisis-management trainer for fire, police, medical and municipal services. Write an approximately 400-word realistic volcanic-eruption incident narrative for professional exercise and decision-support use, based only on the supplied situation. Structure it with activity and hazard footprint, exposed settlements and wind or access conditions, effects on people and infrastructure, response capacity and uncertainty, then the operational picture. If measures are listed, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects, and identify important gaps; distinguish ash, lava and pyroclastic hazards and consider responder exposure. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions. Use clear international British English.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Ashfall and evacuation from an active volcano",
        "desc": "<p>An explosive volcanic eruption is producing intermittent ash pulses that are moving towards a town and a regional airport. Activity is escalating, and the mapped hazard footprint is approaching populated areas, although the most dangerous pyroclastic currents remain within the exclusion zone. Routes are open but a light ash layer is reducing visibility.</p><p>Residents report eye and breathing irritation, while no serious casualties have been confirmed. Local responders remain available, but changing winds could rapidly affect access, aviation and water supplies. Volcanologists are updating forecasts and the civil protection authority is reviewing evacuation triggers.</p><p>Measures selected include managed evacuation routes from the exclusion zone, respiratory protection for ash-exposed responders, respiratory assessment points, ash and air-quality monitoring, suspension of airport operations if ash reaches the approach area, multi-agency command and hazard-specific public alerts. The incident team is maintaining clear thresholds for escalation and shelter advice.</p>",
