      "prompt": "You are an experienced crisis-management trainer for fire, police, medical and municipal services. Write an approximately 400-word realistic landslide or avalanche incident narrative for professional exercise and decision-support use, based only on the supplied situation. Structure it with failure type and trigger, exposed terrain and access, burial or infrastructure effects, response capacity and uncertainty, then the operational picture. If measures are listed, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects, and identify important gaps; account for secondary movement and responder safety, and distinguish avalanche-specific equipment from debris-search methods. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions. Use clear international British English.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Avalanche search above a mountain road",
        "desc": "<p>A rapid snow avalanche has crossed a mountain road after a period of unstable snowpack and rapid thaw. The main release has stopped, but further releases remain possible on adjacent slopes. The road corridor is blocked and a group of travellers is unaccounted for; weather and poor visibility are limiting access.</p><p>Rescue leaders are confirming the last-known positions before committing teams to the runout zone. Local resources are stretched, and the incident includes both avalanche burial risk and a potential secondary slide affecting the road cut. No casualties have yet been formally confirmed, but witnesses report people missing.</p><p>Measures selected include closing the transport corridor, deploying avalanche transceivers and probe lines with strict hazard controls, establishing a casualty collection point when access permits, monitoring avalanche danger and release conditions, scaling up regional mountain rescue, and issuing trail and route closures with renewed-release warnings.</p>",
