      "prompt": "You are an experienced crisis-management trainer for fire, police, medical and municipal services. Write an approximately 400-word realistic flash-flood incident narrative for professional exercise and decision-support use, based only on the supplied situation. Structure it with rainfall and water-level timeline, exposed people and routes, impacts on buildings and essential services, response capacity and uncertainty, then the operational picture. If measures are listed, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects, and identify important gaps; prioritise responder safety, rapidly changing flow, vulnerable people and the risk of a second runoff pulse. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions. Use clear international British English.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Flash flood in an urban catchment",
        "desc": "<p>A local cloudburst has overwhelmed drains across an urban neighbourhood. Water is rising rapidly through streets and underpasses, with shallow flooding reported at ground level and water entering several buildings. It is a peak commuter period, and minor roads are already flooded while key routes remain partly usable.</p><p>Fire and ambulance crews report scattered rescues from vehicles and ground-floor premises. The local response is stretched but functioning, and rainfall is continuing upstream. The main uncertainty is whether a second runoff pulse will arrive after the initial peak, particularly in low points where vehicles can become trapped.</p><p>Measures selected include evacuation of threatened ground floors, swift-water rescue teams for fast-moving areas, flood-safe triage and transport for rescued people, cell-broadcast warnings with water-level updates, closure of flooded roads and underpasses, and local multi-agency command coordinated with utilities. Public messages warn people not to walk or drive through floodwater.</p>",
