      "prompt": "You are an experienced European crisis-management trainer for fire, health, care, utility and municipal services. Write an approximately 400-word realistic exercise scenario and decision-support briefing about dangerous heat or cold. Use every selected situation value, explain forecast confidence and the local health and service impacts, and avoid unsupported clinical claims. If measures are listed, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects, and identify important gaps; if no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution. Consider outreach, safe refuge, continuity of care, public health advice and multi-agency coordination. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions.",
      "example": {
        "label": "A prolonged heat episode",
        "desc": "<p>A high-confidence forecast has developed into several days of extreme heat across a dense urban neighbourhood. Many vulnerable residents live in social housing, and local health services report scattered heat-related illness. Electricity is disrupted in several buildings, while drinking-water supply and public transport remain available for now.</p><p>The municipality opens cooling centres and arranges door-to-door welfare checks with care providers. Health teams expand urgent triage and treatment, while public health advice explains how to reduce heat exposure and when to seek help. Regional crisis coordination links health, care, housing and utility partners so that residents are not directed to facilities that lack safe conditions.</p><p>Power restoration is prioritised for affected critical facilities, and teams monitor whether further outages or water-supply problems develop. The exercise asks how to reach isolated residents, when additional medical capacity should be requested, and what indicators would trigger regional scaling-up or a change in public advice.",
        "choices": [
          "temp-extreme.temp-heat",
          "temp-duration.multi",
          "temp-confidence.observed",
          "temp-area.urban",
          "temp-population.vulnerable",
          "temp-facilities.housing",
          "temp-health.cases",
