      "template": "Drought drivers: {1}; {2}; {3}.\nExposure and context: {4}; {5}; {6}.\nImpacts and capacity: {7}; {8}; {9}.",
      "prompt": "You are an experienced crisis-management trainer for fire, police, medical, environmental and municipal services. Write an approximately 400-word, realistic drought incident narrative for a professional exercise and decision-support briefing, using only the selected situation factors as facts. Establish the timeline, location, affected people and critical services, uncertainty, operational conditions, capacity pressures and likely near-term developments in a clear, calm, evidence-aware tone. Distinguish confirmed information from assumptions and avoid sensationalism. If response measures are listed, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies, coordination needs, side effects and important gaps; do not treat a listed measure as already successful. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and its effects and do not invent or recommend a solution. Structure the response with a concise incident overview, operational picture, key uncertainties and implications. End with exactly two useful reflection or exercise questions for the participants.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Drought incident example",
        "desc": "<p>After several months of below-normal rainfall, a regional reservoir and river system is approaching its seasonal minimum during peak summer. The drought is established across a mixed rural and urban area; drinking-water service remains available but restrictions are in force. Several farms report crop losses, while care homes and a hospital have requested continuity checks as temperatures rise.</p><p>The supply network is single-source, with limited ability to balance flows locally, but demand remains high and forecasts offer little reliable rainfall. Fire danger is elevated on dry heathland near settlements. Water managers are coordinating daily with agriculture, health and municipal teams; current reports distinguish measured reservoir levels from uncertain private-well conditions.</p><p>Measures under consideration include tanker refill points, a non-essential use ban, welfare checks at care homes, irrigation curtailment, transfers between networks and ignition restrictions with patrols. Authorities must explain where restrictions apply, protect essential services and review impacts on livestock, livelihoods and environmental flows as conditions change.</p>",
        "choices": [
          "rainfall-deficit.seasonal-deficit",
          "water-system.rivers-and-reservoirs",
          "drought-phase.established",
          "season.peak-summer",
          "area-type.mixed-rural-and-urban",
          "supply-network.single-source",
          "drinking-water.use-restrictions",
          "agriculture-and-ecology.widespread-losses",
          "wildfire-conditions.elevated",
          "emergency-supply.tanker-refill-points",
