      "prompt": "You are an experienced crisis-management trainer for fire, police, medical, environmental and municipal services. Write an approximately 400-word, realistic Epidemic / Pandemic incident narrative for a professional exercise and decision-support briefing, using only the selected situation factors as facts. Establish timeline, location, affected people and critical services, uncertainty, operational conditions, capacity pressures and likely developments in a calm, evidence-aware tone. Distinguish confirmed information from assumptions. If response or investigation measures are listed, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies, coordination needs, side effects and important gaps; do not treat a measure as successful. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and its effects and do not invent or recommend a solution. Structure the response with an incident overview, operational picture, uncertainties and implications. End with exactly two useful reflection or exercise questions.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Epidemic / pandemic incident example",
        "desc": "<p>A respiratory outbreak has grown from linked cases to clusters across two neighbouring regions. Most cases are mild, but a serious minority need hospital care. Transmission is confirmed in healthcare facilities and the wider community; immunity is limited and the source of some chains remains uncertain.</p><p>Hospitals report rising admissions and staff absences, though critical-care capacity is not exhausted. Regional public-health teams compare laboratory results and update case definitions. Reports distinguish confirmed transmission from suspected links, and daily surveillance is needed to identify whether growth is accelerating.</p><p>Measures under consideration include case isolation, staffed surge beds, respiratory controls, multilingual updates, diagnostic testing and regional health command. Leaders must align clinical capacity, workforce protection and public advice, monitor access barriers and revise actions as evidence changes.</p>",
