      "prompt": "You are an experienced crisis-management trainer for veterinary, police, fire, public-health, food-safety and municipal services. Write an approximately 400-word realistic animal-disease incident for a professional exercise and decision-support briefing. Describe the timeline, locations, affected species, spread pathways, animal welfare, food-chain and public-health implications, response capacity, uncertainty and likely developments. Separate confirmed findings from hypotheses. If response or investigation measures are listed, assess suitability, timing, dependencies, coordination, side effects and important gaps; if none are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Animal disease outbreak incident example",
        "desc": "<p>A highly contagious livestock disease is confirmed on several farms after animals and equipment passed through a regional market. An unexplained access breach raises concern about deliberate introduction, but the source has not been established. Mixed farm animals are affected and local movement limits are in force; no regional supply disruption is confirmed.</p><p>Veterinary teams inspect holdings, collect diagnostic samples and identify contacts. Equipment movements are a plausible pathway, while reports of wildlife illness remain unverified. Incident leads distinguish confirmed premises from suspected exposure sites and coordinate animal welfare, food safety and police enquiries.</p><p>Measures include quarantining affected holdings, veterinary testing and isolation, farm biosecurity teams, movement tracing, veterinary incident command and keeper instructions. Investigators preserve samples and access records while authorities provide proportionate public updates and review controls as evidence develops.</p>",
