      "template": "The incident involves {1} in {2} form, and the release is {3}.\nThe event is at a {4}; the main exposure route is {5}, threatening {6}.\nThe health impact is {7}, response capacity is {8}, and escalation is {9}.",
      "prompt": "You are an experienced crisis-management trainer for fire, medical, environmental and municipal services. Write a realistic incident narrative of about 400 words for a multi-agency exercise and decision-support briefing, based only on the selected facts about an accidental industrial CBRN release. Use a clear, calm, international British English style. Structure the response with a concise incident overview, a chronological account of release and exposure, the evolving operational picture, and implications for affected people and essential services. Distinguish confirmed information from uncertainty and avoid unsupported technical claims about agent behaviour. If any response measures (including investigation measures, if present) are listed, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects, and name important gaps or conflicting priorities. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution or imply that a response has been selected. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions for the participants, focused on decisions, information needs or coordination.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Uncontained solvent vapour release",
        "desc": "<p>A reactor-side transfer hose fails during a routine solvent delivery at an industrial processing site. A toxic vapour cloud drifts across the service yard, and workers report burning eyes and breathing difficulty. The release is still active and the wind direction is changing, so responders cannot yet define a stable hot-zone boundary. Several casualties need urgent assessment, while the on-site team reports that its own breathing-air capacity is limited.</p><p>Fire and hazardous-materials crews isolate the process, establish a monitored perimeter and guide workers away from the plume. Casualties are triaged at a controlled collection point, with decontamination available before ambulance transport if exposure is confirmed. Chemical protective suits and respiratory protection are used for entry. Incident command requests specialist support and keeps neighbouring facilities informed while air monitoring tracks the plume.</p><p>Public protection is being considered for the adjacent road and workplaces as the plume shifts. The account does not assume that nearby residents are contaminated, and responders continue to distinguish reported symptoms from confirmed exposure. The exercise can test how command balances rapid evacuation, safe rescue access and changing monitoring results while keeping protective advice consistent.</p>",
        "choices": [
          "agent-class.agent-chemical",
          "release-form.form-gas",
          "release-status.release-active",
          "site-setting.setting-industrial",
          "exposure-pathway.path-air",
