      "template": "The event involves a {1} with {2}, and the source condition is {3}.\nThe incident is at a {4}; exposure is mainly by {5}, affecting {6}.\nReadings indicate {7}, the health impact is {8}, and response capacity is {9}.",
      "prompt": "You are an experienced radiological emergency adviser and crisis-management trainer for fire, medical, nuclear-regulatory and municipal services. Write a realistic incident narrative of about 400 words for an exercise and decision-support briefing, based only on the selected facts about an accidental nuclear or radiological incident. Use international British English and a calm, precise tone. Structure it as an incident overview, a timeline, the radiological and public-health picture, and the decisions facing responders and authorities. Keep contamination, external irradiation and confirmed measurements distinct; do not invent dose values or health outcomes. If any response measures (including investigation measures, if present) are listed, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects, and name important gaps. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions about protective decisions, uncertainty or coordination.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Reactor-site release and downwind monitoring",
        "desc": "<p>A malfunction during maintenance at a power station damages a radioactive-material containment line. Operators confirm a release to the atmosphere, but the quantities and duration are still being assessed. Initial surveys find contamination across parts of the site and readings at several downwind locations. Staff have been accounted for, while communities beyond the boundary await clear advice and regional radiological specialists are being mobilised.</p><p>The operator stabilises the plant and safely shuts down the affected process under technical supervision. Responders establish controlled access, use personal dosimetry and dose limits, and survey their teams with calibrated instruments. People reporting possible exposure are registered and triaged so that specialist medical advice can be prioritised. Multi-agency command aligns monitoring, health, emergency-service and local-authority updates.</p><p>Evacuation of the affected zone is selected as a precaution while measurements are mapped and verified; the narrative does not imply that every downwind resident has received a harmful dose. Authorities explain what is known, what remains uncertain and when advice will be reviewed. The exercise focuses on decisions under uncertainty, the threshold for protective action and how to maintain trust as monitoring information changes.</p>",
        "choices": [
          "event-source.source-reactor",
          "radiation-hazard.hazard-contamination",
          "release-condition.condition-release",
          "incident-setting.setting-power",
          "exposure-route.route-plume",
          "exposed-area.area-downwind",
          "radiation-level.level-widespread",
          "health-impact.impact-exposure",
          "response-capacity.capacity-national",
          "public-protection.measure-evacuate",
          "medical-response.measure-triage",
          "responder-safety.measure-dosimetry",
          "source-stabilisation.measure-shutdown",
          "environmental-monitoring.measure-monitor",
          "command-information.measure-command"
        ]
      }
