      "template": "An air accident involving {1}, aircraft class {2}, has the status {3}.\nThe crash is in {4}, with {5} and {6}.\nThe impact includes {7}, hazards are {8}, and response capacity is {9}.",
      "prompt": "You are an experienced crisis-management trainer for fire, police, medical, transport and municipal services. Write for incident commanders and crisis-management professionals. Create a realistic, approximately 400-word air-transport accident incident scenario for an exercise or decision-support briefing, based only on the selected situation details and any measures listed. Structure it as: initial situation and timeline; operational challenges and uncertainty; likely short-term developments and dependencies; coordination and communications; and a concise assessment. If measures are listed, including investigation measures if applicable, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects, and name important gaps without assuming they have already succeeded. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution or response plan. Keep the account plausible, neutral and suitable for an international European audience. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions for participants.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Airliner runway excursion",
        "desc": "<p>A regional passenger aircraft overruns the runway during heavy rain at a busy coastal airport in the early evening. The aircraft stops beyond the paved surface with smoke from one engine; airport rescue crews report a localised fuel-fed fire and several people still inside. The wet ground and restricted perimeter road slow access, while the tower suspends arrivals and diverts incoming flights.</p><p>Initial triage identifies multiple injuries but no confirmed fatalities. Airport responders request regional medical reinforcement and establish a casualty collection point. Nearby terminal staff are told to remain indoors until smoke direction is confirmed; the airport emergency centre coordinates rescue, ambulance, police and airline family support.</p><p>Fire crews apply foam to the burning engine area and contain run-off away from surface water. Investigators are given a liaison channel after rescue priorities are met. Participants must manage passenger accountability, family communications, diverted traffic and safe access for specialist teams as the weather continues to affect operations.</p>",
        "choices": [
          "air-event.runway-excursion",
          "aircraft-class.regional-airliner",
          "aircraft-state.fire-active",
          "air-setting.airport-grounds",
          "air-weather.heavy-rain",
