      "template": "A rail incident involving {1} affects a {2}; the train is {3}.\nThe scene is {4}, under {5}, with {6}.\nThe impact includes {7}, cargo status is {8}, and response capacity is {9}.",
      "prompt": "You are an experienced crisis-management trainer for fire, police, medical, transport and municipal services. Write for incident commanders and crisis-management professionals. Create a realistic, approximately 400-word rail transport accident incident scenario for an exercise or decision-support briefing, based only on the selected situation details and any measures listed. Structure it as: initial situation and timeline; operational challenges and uncertainty; likely short-term developments and dependencies; coordination and communications; and a concise assessment. If measures are listed, including investigation measures if applicable, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects, and name important gaps without assuming they have already succeeded. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution or response plan. Keep the account plausible, neutral and suitable for an international European audience. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions for participants.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Passenger train derailment",
        "desc": "<p>A commuter train derails on an elevated approach to a city station during heavy rain in the late afternoon. Two carriages leave the rails but remain upright, and the train comes to a halt close to an adjacent live track. Passengers report several injuries and smoke from a damaged battery compartment; the nearest trackside access road is blocked by traffic.</p><p>The railway operator stops movements on neighbouring lines and dispatches an electrical team to isolate traction power. Fire and rail rescue crews establish a safe access route while medical teams triage passengers at a nearby station. No dangerous goods are reported, but responders keep the affected area cordoned until the battery hazard is assessed.</p><p>Regional command coordinates the fire service, police, ambulance service, railway operator and municipal transport teams. Passenger information must explain station closures and replacement buses without compromising access for emergency vehicles. The exercise explores casualty accountability, safe evacuation, service disruption and the transition from rescue to technical recovery.</p>",
        "choices": [
          "rail-mechanism.derailment",
          "rail-service.commuter-passenger-train",
          "rail-state.train-partially-off-track",
          "rail-setting.open-urban-station",
          "rail-weather.heavy-rain",
          "rail-access.station-road-access",
          "rail-casualties.small-number-injured",
          "rail-cargo.passenger-only-service",
          "rail-capacity.specialist-reinforcement-needed",
          "rail-rescue.rail-rescue-and-fire-crews",
          "rail-medical.on-scene-triage-teams",
          "rail-protective.evacuate-affected-train",
          "rail-stabilise.isolate-traction-power",
          "rail-coordination.coordinate-with-railway-operator",
          "rail-information.announce-replacement-transport"
        ]
      }
    },
