      "template": "A road incident involving {1} affects {2}; vehicles are {3}.\nThe location is {4}, under {5}, with {6}.\nThe impact includes {7}, secondary hazards are {8}, and response capacity is {9}.",
      "prompt": "You are an experienced crisis-management trainer for fire, police, medical, transport and municipal services. Write for incident commanders and crisis-management professionals. Create a realistic, approximately 400-word road transport accident incident scenario for an exercise or decision-support briefing, based only on the selected situation details and any measures listed. Structure it as: initial situation and timeline; operational challenges and uncertainty; likely short-term developments and dependencies; coordination and communications; and a concise assessment. If measures are listed, including investigation measures if applicable, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects, and name important gaps without assuming they have already succeeded. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution or response plan. Keep the account plausible, neutral and suitable for an international European audience. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions for participants.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Coach and car collision",
        "desc": "<p>A coach and several cars collide on a motorway during heavy rain shortly before the evening peak. The coach remains upright but blocks two lanes; several passengers are injured, and one person is trapped in a car. A damaged electric car is smoking, while traffic queues are rapidly extending towards an interchange.</p><p>Fire and road rescue crews secure the vehicles and begin triage. Police close the affected carriageway and divert approaching traffic before secondary collisions occur. Ambulance teams establish a casualty collection point, and the road authority prepares a safe route for emergency vehicles and replacement public transport.</p><p>Multi-agency incident command coordinates rescue, medical care, traffic control and communication with the public. Responders assess the electric vehicle battery and confirm whether the smoke presents a continuing hazard. The exercise tests prioritisation, casualty accountability, congestion management and the handover from rescue operations to road clearance.</p>",
        "choices": [
          "road-mechanism.multi-vehicle-collision",
          "road-vehicle.bus-or-coach",
          "road-state.entrapment-reported",
