      "template": "A maritime incident involving {1} affects a {2}; the vessel is {3}.\nThe water setting is {4}, with {5}, at {6}.\nPeople at risk include {7}, cargo or pollution concerns are {8}, and response capacity is {9}.",
      "prompt": "You are an experienced crisis-management trainer for fire, police, medical, transport and municipal services. Write for incident commanders and crisis-management professionals. Create a realistic, approximately 400-word maritime transport accident incident scenario for an exercise or decision-support briefing, based only on the selected situation details and any measures listed. Structure it as: initial situation and timeline; operational challenges and uncertainty; likely short-term developments and dependencies; coordination and communications; and a concise assessment. If measures are listed, including investigation measures if applicable, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects, and name important gaps without assuming they have already succeeded. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution or response plan. Keep the account plausible, neutral and suitable for an international European audience. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions for participants.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Ferry engine-room fire",
        "desc": "<p>A passenger ferry suffers an engine-room fire while approaching a coastal port in strong winds. The vessel remains afloat but has lost propulsion and is drifting towards a shoal. Smoke is contained below deck, and crew members report a small number of injuries; all passengers have not yet been accounted for.</p><p>The coastguard coordinates harbour rescue craft and a lifeboat response while the vessel crew fight the fire and prepare for possible evacuation. The port activates its emergency plan, suspends movements in the affected approach channel and establishes a shore casualty reception point. Firefighting water and a possible fuel leak raise concern about pollution near the harbour entrance.</p><p>Incident command must decide whether to keep passengers aboard or transfer them to rescue craft, balancing smoke conditions, wind and the vessel's drift. Public updates cover port and ferry disruption while family assistance arrangements are set up. Participants assess rescue priorities, pollution prevention, command handoffs and the trigger for regional maritime support.</p>",
        "choices": [
          "marine-mechanism.on-board-fire",
          "marine-vessel.passenger-ferry",
          "marine-state.fire-active",
          "marine-water.coastal-waters",
          "marine-weather.strong-wind",
          "marine-proximity.near-accessible-shore",
          "marine-people.people-unaccounted-for",
          "marine-hazard.fuel-leak",
          "marine-capacity.specialist-reinforcement-needed",
          "marine-rescue.lifeboat-and-rescue-teams",
          "marine-medical.shore-casualty-reception-point",
          "marine-protective.abandon-ship-evacuation",
          "marine-stabilise.fight-vessel-fire",
          "marine-coordination.activate-port-emergency-plan",
          "marine-information.advise-port-and-ferry-users"
        ]
      }
    },
