      "template": "The pipeline event is a {1}; gas flow is {2}, with {3} as the main escalation hazard.\nThe incident affects a {4}; gas is dispersing by {5}, near {6}.\nThe casualty impact is {7}, infrastructure impact is {8}, and response capacity is {9}.",
      "prompt": "You are an experienced fire-service incident commander and crisis-management trainer working with gas-network operators, police, ambulance and local authorities. Write a realistic incident narrative of about 400 words for an exercise and decision-support briefing about an accidental pipeline or gas explosion. Use international British English, a calm operational tone and no unsupported technical detail. Structure the narrative as an initial call and incident picture, a short timeline, the risks to people and infrastructure, and the operational decisions that follow. Distinguish a leak, a sustained fire and an explosion, and make clear what is known about gas isolation and dispersion. If any response measures (including investigation measures, if present) are listed, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects, and name important gaps. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions for participants.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Gas accumulation in a residential building",
        "desc": "<p>Residents report a strong gas odour in the basement of a multi-storey residential building. Gas has accumulated in an enclosed service area and the network operator cannot initially confirm whether the building service pipe is isolated. There has been no explosion, but an ignition could cause severe harm. Several occupants report minor injuries during evacuation, and specialist fire and utility teams are requested.</p><p>Fire crews establish a controlled perimeter, warn residents and nearby facilities, and evacuate people from the immediate hazard zone without creating ignition sources. Gas detection teams monitor concentrations on each level and identify safe access routes. The network operator is asked to isolate the supply, while incident command coordinates rescue, ambulance and building-management activity. No one is directed to re-enter until the atmosphere is assessed.</p><p>Once the source is isolated, responders can decide whether specialist-assessed ventilation is safe and how adjacent flats should be checked. Casualties are triaged for appropriate treatment, and regular updates explain the cordon, re-entry criteria and any service disruption. The exercise tests the sequence of isolation, monitoring and public protection when the risk of an explosion is immediate but the source condition is initially uncertain.</p>",
        "choices": [
          "event-type.event-leak",
          "gas-flow.flow-active",
          "escalation-hazard.hazard-accumulation",
          "pipeline-setting.setting-building",
          "gas-dispersion.dispersion-indoor",
          "exposed-area.area-neighbourhood",
          "casualty-impact.impact-injuries",
          "infrastructure-impact.damage-local",
          "response-capacity.capacity-specialist",
          "protect-people.measure-evacuate",
          "fire-control.measure-control-ignition",
          "casualty-care.measure-triage",
          "gas-isolation.measure-utility",
          "hazard-monitoring.measure-detect",
          "command-information.measure-alert"
        ]
      }
    },
