      "template": "A {1} followed {2}, and the remaining structure is {3}.\nThe affected structure is a {4}; access is {5}, and occupancy is {6}.\nThe casualty picture is {7}, community impact is {8}, rescue capacity is {9}, and secondary hazards are {10}.",
      "prompt": "You are an experienced urban search-and-rescue commander and crisis-management trainer for fire, ambulance, police, engineering and municipal services. Write a realistic incident narrative of about 400 words for an exercise and decision-support briefing about an accidental building or structure collapse. Use international British English and a measured operational tone. Structure the response as an initial situation report, a concise timeline, the rescue and secondary-hazard picture, and the coordination decisions ahead. Do not assume people are trapped unless the selected facts say so; distinguish confirmed information from reports and uncertainty. If any response measures (including investigation measures, if present) are listed, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects, and name important gaps. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions about prioritisation, safety or coordination.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Residents trapped after a partial collapse",
        "desc": "<p>A section of an older residential building collapses after an internal structural failure. The remaining structure is unstable, debris blocks part of the street and occupants report that two residents may be trapped in an upper floor. Firefighters confirm one injured person outside while the status of others remains uncertain. The neighbourhood is displaced and urban search-and-rescue support is on its way.</p><p>Command establishes an exclusion zone and a safe staging area, then deploys technical search teams to listen for signs of life before moving debris. Structural engineers assess the remaining walls and prepare shoring options. Utilities are isolated where damaged, and ambulance teams establish a casualty collection point with trauma-hospital capacity coordinated in advance. Residents from adjoining unsafe properties are directed to temporary shelter.</p><p>The incident commander balances the urgency of locating trapped people against the risk of a secondary collapse. Family liaison staff record missing-person reports and provide verified updates without speculating about outcomes. The exercise tests how search, engineering, rescue and community-support decisions change as occupancy information is confirmed and structural monitoring develops.</p>",
        "choices": [
          "collapse-extent.collapse-partial",
          "failure-trigger.trigger-structural",
          "stability.uncertain",
          "structure-setting.setting-residential",
          "access-condition.access-restricted",
          "occupancy.people",
