      "template": "A telecommunications failure caused by {1} affects {2}; the outage trajectory is {3}.\nThe affected area is {4}, with {5} and {6}.\nEmergency communications are {7}, dependent services include {8}, and response capacity is {9}.",
      "prompt": "You are an experienced crisis-management trainer for fire, police, medical, transport and municipal services. Write for incident commanders and crisis-management professionals. Create a realistic, approximately 400-word telecommunications network failure incident scenario for an exercise or decision-support briefing, based only on the selected situation details and any measures listed. Structure it as: initial situation and timeline; operational challenges and uncertainty; likely short-term developments and dependencies; coordination and communications; and a concise assessment. If measures are listed, including investigation measures if applicable, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects, and name important gaps without assuming they have already succeeded. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution or response plan. Keep the account plausible, neutral and suitable for an international European audience. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions for participants.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Regional mobile and fixed network outage",
        "desc": "<p>A software configuration fault in a regional telecoms core network causes intermittent fixed and mobile service loss during a major public event. Some emergency calls fail to route, while dispatch links for several response organisations are degraded. The fault is not yet fully isolated, and the operator warns that service instability may continue while engineers restore the previous configuration.</p><p>Emergency services switch to radio interoperability channels and establish staffed public contact points near the event. Network operators coordinate with emergency-service control rooms to prioritise emergency call routing and confirm which areas have reliable backup coverage. Health and care providers check on clients who normally rely on remote monitoring or telephone support.</p><p>Regional crisis command shares verified updates with local authorities and event organisers, including alternate ways to contact emergency services where applicable. The exercise considers how to protect public access, manage uncertainty in restoration estimates and coordinate when several operators and essential services depend on the same network. Participants identify communications gaps and avoid encouraging unnecessary calls.</p>",
        "choices": [
          "telecom-cause.software-or-configuration-fault",
          "telecom-scope.regional-network-outage",
          "telecom-duration.repairs-underway",
          "telecom-area.dense-urban-area",
          "telecom-demand.major-public-event",
          "telecom-site-access.equipment-site-accessible",
          "telecom-emergency.some-emergency-calls-failing",
          "telecom-services.emergency-services-affected",
          "telecom-capacity.specialist-engineers-needed",
          "telecom-public-access.open-staffed-emergency-contact-points",
          "telecom-continuity.use-satellite-links-for-command",
          "telecom-welfare.open-local-information-point",
          "telecom-repair.roll-back-faulty-configuration",
          "telecom-coordination.coordinate-network-operators-and-emergency-servi",
          "telecom-information.advise-on-alternate-contact-channels"
        ]
      }
    },
