      "template": "The release involves a {1}; the spill is {2} and the source is {3}.\nThe incident is at a {4}; the material is spreading by {5}, threatening {6}.\nThe health impact is {7}, environmental impact is {8}, and response capacity is {9}.",
      "prompt": "You are an experienced hazardous-materials officer and crisis-management trainer for fire, ambulance, environmental, police and local-authority services. Write a realistic incident narrative of about 400 words for an exercise and decision-support briefing about an accidental hazardous-material spill. Use international British English, a clear operational tone and avoid inventing chemical properties not supplied by the selected facts. Structure it as an incident overview, a short timeline, the exposure and environmental pathways, and the decisions facing responders and authorities. Keep confirmed impacts distinct from risks and uncertainties. If any response measures (including investigation measures, if present) are listed, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects, and name important gaps. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions about containment, public protection or coordination.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Toxic vapour leak from a road tanker",
        "desc": "<p>A road tanker is damaged in a low-speed collision on the edge of a busy neighbourhood. A toxic substance is escaping and vapour is moving towards nearby shops and pedestrians. The product name is available from the transport documents, but the exact release rate is not yet known. Several people report irritation without serious symptoms, and local responders request specialist hazardous-materials support.</p><p>Crews establish hot, warm and cold zones, monitor gas concentrations and evacuate the immediate hazard area. Responders use chemical protective clothing and breathing apparatus, while medical teams triage potentially exposed people and provide substance-specific clinical advice. The leak is stopped only when a safe approach is confirmed. Environmental teams collect air samples to track the plume and assess whether conditions are changing.</p><p>Multi-agency command coordinates traffic control, ambulance access, environmental monitoring and public updates. Nearby businesses receive clear instructions about where to move and what symptoms to report. No waterway contamination is assumed in this account; the exercise asks participants to reassess measures if monitoring reveals a different spread pathway or if the tanker cannot be isolated.</p>",
        "choices": [
          "material-hazard.material-toxic",
          "spill-scale.spill-local",
          "release-status.release-active",
          "incident-setting.setting-road",
          "spread-pathway.path-air",
          "exposure.public",
          "health-impact.impact-exposure",
          "environmental-impact.environment-local",
          "response-capacity.capacity-specialist",
          "protect-people.measure-evacuate",
          "casualty-care.measure-triage",
          "responder-safety.measure-ppe-chemical",
          "source-control.measure-stop",
          "environmental-protection.measure-sample",
          "command-information.measure-command"
        ]
      }
    },
