      "template": "Incident profile: {1}; {2}; {3}.\nLocation and exposure: {4}; {5}; {6}.\nImpact and capacity: {7}; {8}; {9}.",
      "prompt": "You are an experienced crisis-management trainer for fire, police, medical, public-health and municipal services. Write a realistic incident scenario or decision-support briefing of about 400 words for professional European emergency responders, using only the situation information below. Structure it with a concise incident overview, developing impacts and uncertainty, multi-agency response considerations, and consequences for affected people and essential services. Stay at the level of professional response, protection, investigation and lawful apprehension; do not describe operational attack methods. If response or investigation measures are listed, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects, and identify important gaps without treating selections as a guaranteed plan. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions for the team.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Terrorist attack (CBRN) — exercise briefing",
        "desc": "<p>A suspected chemical release is reported at a public venue, with several people experiencing symptoms and the exposure route still under assessment. Responders establish hot, warm and cold zones, request specialist sampling, and organise controlled evacuation, decontamination before clean medical care, exposure registration and hospital coordination. The affected area is isolated, environmental monitoring is maintained and health, fire, police and municipal partners share a common operating picture. Public advice is updated as evidence changes. Investigators preserve access records and samples with chain-of-custody controls, coordinate health and police analysis, and prepare safe lawful suspect-tracing and apprehension measures.</p><p>Incident command maintains proportionate safety controls, confirms responsibilities across agencies and gives accessible, verified information to people who need to act. Partners record decisions and uncertainties so that protective actions can be reviewed as evidence develops.</p><p>After immediate life-safety priorities are addressed, the lead authorities coordinate recovery and a lawful investigation. The team reviews the timing and suitability of selected measures, identifies unmet needs and agrees how to adapt if the threat or impact assessment changes.</p>",
        "choices": [
          "release-status.release-confirmed",
          "hazard-class.chemical-hazard",
          "exposure-evidence.exposure-confirmed",
          "setting.transport-or-public-venue",
          "population-presence.high-occupancy",
          "responder-access.access-restricted",
          "casualty-status.injuries-reported",
          "service-impact.local-disruption",
          "response-capacity.resources-stretched",
          "protective-action.controlled-evacuation",
          "life-saving-response.decontaminate-before-clean-medical-care",
          "specialist-protection.establish-hot-warm-and-cold-zones",
          "scene-control.isolate-the-affected-area",
          "command-coordination.activate-multi-agency-command",
          "public-information.issue-immediate-protective-advice",
          "evidence.collect-samples-with-chain-of-custody-controls",
          "suspect-tracing.coordinate-public-health-and-police-analysis",
          "apprehension.plan-safe-controlled-arrest"
        ]
      }
