      "template": "The incident context is {1}, {2} and {3}.\nThe location and exposure are {4}, {5} and {6}.\nThe operational impact is {7}, {8} and {9}.",
      "prompt": "You are an experienced crisis-management trainer for emergency services, infrastructure operators and public authorities. Write a realistic exercise or decision-support briefing of about 400 words for incident commanders and relevant partner leads. Use the selected situation values as the factual frame, distinguish confirmed information from uncertainty, and structure the account as incident onset, context and exposure, effects on people and services, response pressures, and near-term outlook. Keep the account at professional response-planning level and do not provide instructions that would enable harm. If measures (including investigation measures) are listed, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects and identify important gaps. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution. End with two reflection or exercise questions.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Critical infrastructure sabotage exercise",
        "desc": "<p>A developing critical infrastructure sabotage incident is characterised by Physical damage, Energy installation, Confirmed active incident, Dense urban site, Public access nearby, Regional network link, Major service loss, Several local assets, Specialist resources strained. Early reports do not yet establish its full scope or cause. The incident lead verifies immediate exposure with service owners and partner agencies, records confirmed facts separately from working assumptions, and protects people who may be at risk.</p><p>Response teams maintain a shared picture of impacts, dependencies and available capacity. They select Evacuate immediate hazard zone, Inspect and stabilise the asset, Activate alternative service provision, Scale up to regional coordination, Share a common inter-agency situation report, Publish verified service disruption updates to support safe operations and communicate verified information through appropriate channels. Measures are sequenced as conditions change, with attention to service continuity, evidence integrity and public confidence.</p><p>Investigation measures include Preserve relevant records and access logs, Refer attribution assessment to police specialists, Support lawful intervention and suspect apprehension. Competent authorities coordinate attribution and lawful tracing without premature conclusions, and pursue apprehension, disruption or prosecution through appropriate routes. The exercise tests how operational leaders balance immediate protection, restoration and accountability as new information emerges.</p>",
        "choices": [
          "sab-event.1",
          "sab-target.1",
          "sab-status.2",
          "sab-setting.1",
          "sab-exposure.3",
          "sab-dependency.2",
          "sab-impact.2",
          "sab-scale.2",
          "sab-capacity.2",
          "sab-life-safety.1",
          "sab-contain.2",
          "sab-continuity.1",
          "sab-command.2",
          "sab-liaison.2",
          "sab-warning.2",
          "sab-evidence.2",
          "sab-trace.1",
          "sab-enforce.1"
        ]
      }
    },
