      "template": "Incident profile: {1}; {2}; {3}.\nLocation and exposure: {4}; {5}; {6}.\nImpact and capacity: {7}; {8}; {9}.",
      "prompt": "You are an experienced crisis-management trainer for fire, police, medical, public-health and municipal services. Write a realistic incident scenario or decision-support briefing of about 400 words for professional European emergency responders, using only the situation information below. Structure it with a concise incident overview, developing impacts and uncertainty, multi-agency response considerations, and consequences for affected people and essential services. Stay at the level of professional response, protection, investigation and lawful apprehension; do not describe operational attack methods. If response or investigation measures are listed, assess their suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects, and identify important gaps without treating selections as a guaranteed plan. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions for the team.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Hostage / Kidnapping situation — exercise briefing",
        "desc": "<p>Police receive credible information that a person is being held against their will at a confirmed location; the suspect is outstanding and the person’s condition is not yet known, and injuries have been reported among nearby people. A workplace and neighbourhood are affected, so command arranges discreet safeguards, trauma-informed family liaison and specialist crisis-negotiation and safeguarding support. Police-led multi-agency command controls access while protecting safe routes and personal details. Approved updates are shared only as appropriate, with rumours monitored carefully. Investigators preserve access records, coordinate witness accounts and urgent person-tracing enquiries, and share intelligence lawfully with relevant partners. Any suspect intervention is planned under police authority with safety, custody and evidence continuity in view.</p><p>Incident command maintains proportionate safety controls, confirms responsibilities across agencies and gives accessible, verified information to people who need to act. Partners record decisions and uncertainties so that protective actions can be reviewed as evidence develops.</p><p>After immediate life-safety priorities are addressed, the lead authorities coordinate recovery and a lawful investigation. The team reviews the timing and suitability of selected measures, identifies unmet needs and agrees how to adapt if the threat or impact assessment changes.</p>",
        "choices": [
          "incident-status.person-confirmed-held-or-unlocated",
          "location-confidence.location-confirmed",
          "suspect-status.suspect-outstanding",
          "setting.residential-neighbourhood",
          "population-presence.low-occupancy",
          "responder-access.access-restricted",
          "casualty-status.injuries-reported",
          "service-impact.local-disruption",
          "response-capacity.resources-stretched",
          "protective-action.shelter-nearby-people-in-a-secure-place",
          "life-saving-response.arrange-trauma-informed-welfare-support",
          "specialist-protection.activate-specialist-crisis-negotiation-support",
          "scene-control.secure-the-relevant-area-discreetly",
          "command-coordination.activate-multi-agency-command",
          "public-information.manage-rumours-and-protect-personal-details",
          "evidence.preserve-relevant-locations-and-access-records",
          "suspect-tracing.coordinate-urgent-missing-person-enquiries",
          "apprehension.plan-safe-controlled-arrest"
        ]
