      "template": "The {1} has reported {2} in {3}.\nThose at risk are {4}, nearby traffic is {5}, and sea conditions are {6}.\nThe crew are {7}; the vessel is {8}, and response capacity is {9}.",
      "prompt": "You are an experienced maritime crisis-management trainer for coastguard, port, police, medical and shipping partners. Write an approximately 400-word professional British-English incident narrative for an exercise and decision-support briefing, using all selected situation values without contradicting them. Describe chronology, operating area, people at risk, vessel condition, uncertainty, jurisdictional considerations and response capacity; distinguish verified information from reports. If protection, maritime-coordination or investigation measures are listed, assess suitability, timing, dependencies and side effects, and identify important gaps. Prioritise life safety, safe navigation, applicable maritime law and proportionate cross-agency coordination. If no measures are listed, describe only the incident and do not invent a solution. Structure the account as a situation overview, developing impacts and decision points, then an assessment. End with exactly two reflection or exercise questions.",
      "example": {
        "label": "Hijacking report in coastal waters",
        "desc": "<p>A cargo vessel in coastal waters reports a suspected boarding attempt during moderate traffic. The crew is accounted for but several people are distressed, and the vessel has stopped while the master seeks assistance. Conditions are favourable, and the local coastguard is available to coordinate a response with port and flag authorities.</p><p>Responders establish secure communications and account for all persons aboard, while medical advice is arranged. The competent maritime authority shares verified position updates and issues a proportionate navigation warning. The port prepares a controlled reception option, and operators receive confirmed information rather than speculation.</p><p>Once the situation is safe, relevant navigation records and witness accounts are preserved. Authorities coordinate cross-border enquiries and refer any suspected offenders to the competent law-enforcement body under applicable jurisdiction. The exercise focuses on crew safety, continuity of navigation and a lawful, evidence-led investigation.</p>",
        "choices": [
          "vessel-type.cargo-vessel",
          "incident-stage.attempted-boarding-reported",
          "operating-area.coastal-or-territorial-waters",
          "people-at-risk.crew-only",
          "traffic-density.moderate-coastal-traffic",
